Code Planning:
	Create grid:
		- Use rendertarget to scale up screen and scale mouse down respectively.
		- Lock to 960x540
		- Use a large array of tile structs to organize data. I think a 1-dimensional array
		will make the most sense
	Conveyers:
		- Check if mouse is down and if so, change a boolean field of the selected
		tile struct to true, unless of course, a tile is already active. Inverse
		action for destroying a belt. Right click to
		create a conveyer, left click to destroy, middle click or space bar to
		change the direction
		- Each tile struct will also have a direction ENUM? that tells the fireworks
		which direction they'll move. 4 directions obviously.
	Fireworks:
		- Will take their position based off of the middle of their hitbox, and will
		move accordingly from tile to tile.
		- There will be a firework struct, along with an array the holds all the fireworks.
		- This array will not be dynamically allocated, rather there will be a maximum
		number of fireworks (the number of tiles on the screen) and they will have
		a boolean field that stores whether or not the firework is actively moving.
		- Along with the obvious two position fields, the firework struct will have
		another boolean field, that decides if it's a bomb or not. This field will be
		reset each time the firework resets back to spawn, and will be set randomly with
		a to-be-determined probability after the first several fireworks of the level.
		- *** I should be able to just loop through the array of fireworks to find which
		are active and should be rendered, but it would be a good exercise post-jam to
		optimize this. ***
